1
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:12,330
Mr. Vice President, Governor, Congressman Thomas, Senator Wiley and Congressman Miller,

2
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:21,210
Mr. Webb, Mr. Bell, scientists, distinguished guests, and ladies and gentlemen, I appreciate

3
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:27,690
your President having made me an honorary visiting professor and I will assure you that my first

4
00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:33,049
lecture will be very brief. I am delighted to be here and I'm particularly delighted

5
00:00:33,049 --> 00:00:42,530
to be here on this occasion. We meet at a college, noted for knowledge, in a city, noted for progress,

6
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:48,969
in a state, noted for strength, and we stand in need of all three. For we meet in an hour

7
00:00:48,969 --> 00:00:56,530
of change and challenge, in a decade of hope and fear, in an age of both knowledge and

8
00:00:56,530 --> 00:01:03,869
ignorance. The greater our knowledge increases, the greater our ignorance unfolds. Despite

9
00:01:03,869 --> 00:01:10,849
the striking fact that most of the scientists that the world has ever known are alive and

10
00:01:10,849 --> 00:01:18,010
working today, despite the fact that this nation's own scientific manpower is doubling

11
00:01:18,010 --> 00:01:25,129
every 12 years , a rate of growth, more than three times that of our population as

12
00:01:25,129 --> 00:01:34,010
a whole, despite that, the vast stretches of the unknown and the unanswered and the

13
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:43,090
unfinished still far outstrip our collective comprehension. No man can fully grasp how

14
00:01:43,090 --> 00:01:53,290
far and how fast we have come, but condense, if you will, the 50,000 years of man's recorded

15
00:01:53,290 --> 00:02:00,969
history in a time span of about a half a century. Stated in these terms, we know very

16
00:02:00,969 --> 00:02:07,049
little about the first 40 years, except at the end of them, advanced man had learned

17
00:02:07,049 --> 00:02:14,569
to use the skins of animals to cover them. Then about 10 years ago, under this standard,

18
00:02:14,569 --> 00:02:21,889
man emerged from his caves to construct other kinds of shelter. Only five years ago, man

19
00:02:21,889 --> 00:02:30,050
learned to write and use a cart with wheels. Christianity began less than two years ago.

20
00:02:30,050 --> 00:02:37,169
The printing press came this year, and then less than two months ago, during this whole

21
00:02:37,169 --> 00:02:45,169
50-year span of human history, the steam engine provided a new source of power. Newton explored

22
00:02:45,169 --> 00:02:52,689
the meaning of gravity. Last month, electric lights and telephones and automobiles and

23
00:02:52,689 --> 00:03:00,530
airplanes became available. Only last week did we develop penicillin and television and nuclear

24
00:03:00,530 --> 00:03:09,009
power. And now, if America's new spacecraft succeeds in reaching Venus, we will have literally

25
00:03:09,009 --> 00:03:16,449
reached the stars before midnight tonight. This is a breathtaking pace, and such a pace

26
00:03:16,449 --> 00:03:26,610
cannot help but create new ills as it dispels old. New ignorance, new problems, new dangers.

27
00:03:26,610 --> 00:03:35,330
Surely the opening vistas of space promise high costs and hardships as well as high reward.

28
00:03:35,409 --> 00:03:42,849
So it is not surprising that some would have us stay where we are a little longer, to rest,

29
00:03:42,849 --> 00:03:50,610
to wait. But this city of Houston, this state of Texas, this country of the United States was not

30
00:03:50,610 --> 00:04:05,469
built by those who waited and rested and wished to look behind them. This country was conquered

31
00:04:06,430 --> 00:04:15,789
by those who moved forward, and so will space. William Bradford, speaking in 1630 of the

32
00:04:15,789 --> 00:04:22,589
founding of the Plymouth Bay Colony, said that all great and honorable actions are accompanied

33
00:04:22,589 --> 00:04:30,189
with great difficulties, and both must be enterprised and overcome with answerable courage.

34
00:04:31,069 --> 00:04:38,110
If this capsule history of our progress teaches us, it is, that man in his quest for knowledge and

35
00:04:38,110 --> 00:04:45,709
progress is determined and cannot be deterred. The exploration of space will go ahead, whether we

36
00:04:45,709 --> 00:04:54,110
join in it or not. And it is one of the great adventures of all time and no nation which

37
00:04:54,110 --> 00:05:00,509
expects to be the leader of other nations can expect to stay behind in this race for space.

38
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:08,269
Those who came in before us made certain that this country rode the first waves of the

39
00:05:08,269 --> 00:05:14,990
industrial revolution, the first waves of modern invention, and the first wave of nuclear power,

40
00:05:15,629 --> 00:05:23,149
and this generation does not intend to founder in the backwash of the coming age of space.

41
00:05:23,149 --> 00:05:26,430
We mean to be a part of it. We mean to lead it.

42
00:05:26,430 --> 00:05:44,699
The eyes of the world now look into space to the moon and to the planets beyond,

43
00:05:45,579 --> 00:05:52,860
and we have vowed that we shall not see it governed by a hostile flag of conquest,

44
00:05:53,740 --> 00:06:00,139
but by a banner of freedom and peace. We have vowed that we shall not see space

45
00:06:01,100 --> 00:06:06,939
filled with weapons of mass destruction, but with instruments of knowledge and understanding,

46
00:06:07,899 --> 00:06:15,420
yet the vows of this nation can only be fulfilled if we in this nation are first,

47
00:06:16,220 --> 00:06:31,220
and therefore we intend to be first. Our leadership in science and industry, our hopes

48
00:06:31,300 --> 00:06:39,379
for peace and security, our obligations to ourselves as well as others all require us to make this

49
00:06:39,379 --> 00:06:47,300
effort to solve these mysteries, to solve them for the good of all men, and to become the world's

50
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:55,220
leading space-faring nation. We set sail on this new sea because there is new knowledge to

51
00:06:55,220 --> 00:07:02,980
be gained and new rights to be won, and they must be won and used for the progress of all people.

52
00:07:03,939 --> 00:07:10,339
For space science, like nuclear science and all technology, has no conscience of its own.

53
00:07:11,459 --> 00:07:18,259
Whether it will become a force for good or ill depends on man, and only if the United States

54
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:28,579
occupies a position of preeminence can we help decide whether this new ocean will be a sea of

55
00:07:28,579 --> 00:07:37,699
peace or a new terrifying theater of war. I do not say that we should or will go unprotected

56
00:07:38,500 --> 00:07:45,379
against the hostile misuse of space any more than we go unprotected against the hostile use

57
00:07:45,379 --> 00:07:53,300
of land or sea, but I do say that space can be explored and mastered without feeding the

58
00:07:53,300 --> 00:07:59,540
fires of war, without repeating the mistakes that man has made in extending his writ

59
00:07:59,540 --> 00:08:07,699
around this globe of ours. There is no strife, no prejudice, no national conflict in outer space

60
00:08:07,699 --> 00:08:15,459
as yet. Its hazards are hostile to us all. Its conquest deserves the best of all mankind,

61
00:08:16,339 --> 00:08:23,459
and its opportunity for peaceful cooperation may never come again. But why, some say, the moon?

62
00:08:24,420 --> 00:08:30,819
Why choose this as our goal? And they may well ask, why climb the highest mountain?

63
00:08:31,779 --> 00:08:39,299
Why 35 years ago fly the Atlantic? Why does Rice play Texas? We choose to go to the moon.

64
00:08:40,500 --> 00:08:52,750
We choose to go to the moon, choose to go to the moon in this decade and do the other things,

65
00:08:52,750 --> 00:09:00,269
not because they are easy, but because they are hard, because that goal will serve to organize

66
00:09:00,269 --> 00:09:06,029
and measure the best of our energies and skills, because that challenge is one that

67
00:09:06,029 --> 00:09:12,830
we're willing to accept, one we are unwilling to postpone, and one we intend to win, and the

68
00:09:12,830 --> 00:09:27,549
others too. For these reasons, I regard the decision last year to shift our efforts in space

69
00:09:28,350 --> 00:09:32,909
from low to high gear as among the most important decisions that will be made

70
00:09:33,549 --> 00:09:38,350
during my incumbency in the office of the presidency. In the last 24 hours,

71
00:09:39,070 --> 00:09:45,789
we have seen facilities now being created for the greatest and most complex exploration in man's

72
00:09:45,789 --> 00:09:53,389
history. We have felt the ground shake and the air shattered by the testing of a Saturn C-1

73
00:09:53,389 --> 00:10:01,389
booster rocket, many times as powerful as the Atlas which launched John Glenn, generating power

74
00:10:01,389 --> 00:10:07,950
equivalents to 10,000 automobiles with their accelerator on the floor. We have seen the site

75
00:10:08,750 --> 00:10:16,590
where five F-1 rocket engines, each one as powerful as all engines of the Saturn combined,

76
00:10:17,309 --> 00:10:24,350
would be clustered together to make the advanced Saturn missile assembled in a new building

77
00:10:25,070 --> 00:10:32,669
to be built at Cape Canaveral, as tall as a 48-story structure, as wide as a city block,

78
00:10:33,309 --> 00:10:41,789
and as long as two lengths of this field. Within these last 19 months, at least 45 satellites

79
00:10:41,789 --> 00:10:46,750
have circled the earth. Some 40 of them were made in the United States of America,

80
00:10:47,470 --> 00:10:53,389
and they were far more sophisticated and supplied far more knowledge to the people of the world

81
00:10:53,389 --> 00:11:05,919
than those of the Soviet Union. The Mariner spacecraft now on its way to Venus

82
00:11:06,639 --> 00:11:12,639
is the most intricate instrument in the history of space science. The accuracy of that shot

83
00:11:13,360 --> 00:11:18,559
is comparable to firing a missile from Cape Canaveral and dropping it in this stadium

84
00:11:18,559 --> 00:11:25,679
between the 40-yard lines. Transit satellites are helping our ships at sea to steer a safer course.

85
00:11:26,559 --> 00:11:32,399
Tiros satellites have given us unprecedented warnings of hurricanes and storms, and we'll do

86
00:11:32,399 --> 00:11:38,559
the same for forest fires and icebergs. We have had our failures, but so have others,

87
00:11:38,559 --> 00:11:49,279
even if they do not admit them, and they may be less public. To be sure, to be sure we are

88
00:11:49,279 --> 00:11:57,039
behind, and we'll be behind for some time in man's flight, but we do not intend to stay behind,

89
00:11:57,039 --> 00:12:10,720
and in this decade we shall make up and move ahead. The growth of our science and education

90
00:12:11,519 --> 00:12:14,720
will be enriched by new knowledge of our universe and environment,

91
00:12:15,519 --> 00:12:21,519
by new techniques of learning and mapping and observations, by new tools and computers

92
00:12:21,519 --> 00:12:27,360
for industry, medicine, and the home as well as the school. Technical institutions such as

93
00:12:27,360 --> 00:12:33,519
Rice will reap the harvest of these gains. And finally, the space effort itself,

94
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:38,960
while still in its infancy, has already created a great number of new companies

95
00:12:39,600 --> 00:12:46,960
and tens of thousands of new jobs. Space and related industries are generating new demands

96
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:53,279
in investment and skilled personnel, and this city, and this state, and this region

97
00:12:53,919 --> 00:13:00,480
will share greatly in this role. What was once the furthest outpost on the old frontier of the West

98
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:13,409
will be the furthest outpost on the new frontier of science and space. Houston, your city of Houston,

99
00:13:13,970 --> 00:13:21,009
with its manned spacecraft center, will become the heart of a large scientific and engineering

100
00:13:21,009 --> 00:13:27,090
community. During the next five years, the National Aeronautic and Space Administration

101
00:13:27,090 --> 00:13:33,090
expects to double the number of scientists and engineers in this area, to increase its

102
00:13:33,090 --> 00:13:40,610
outlays for salaries and expenses to $60 million a year, to invest some $200 million in plant

103
00:13:40,610 --> 00:13:47,570
and laboratory facilities, and to direct or contract for new space efforts over $1 billion

104
00:13:48,289 --> 00:13:54,289
from this center in this city. To be sure, all this costs us all a good deal of money.

105
00:13:55,009 --> 00:14:01,090
This year's space budget is three times what it was in January 1961,

106
00:14:01,970 --> 00:14:06,129
and it is greater than the space budget of the previous eight years combined.

107
00:14:06,929 --> 00:14:13,809
That budget now stands at $5,400,000,000 a year, a staggering sum,

108
00:14:14,529 --> 00:14:18,610
though somewhat less than we pay for cigarettes and cigars every year.

109
00:14:19,490 --> 00:14:32,419
Space expenditures will soon rise some more, from 40 cents per person per week

110
00:14:33,139 --> 00:14:38,100
to more than 50 cents a week for every man, woman and child in the United States.

111
00:14:39,059 --> 00:14:45,539
For we have given this program a high national priority, even though I realized that this

112
00:14:45,539 --> 00:14:52,980
is, in some measure, an act of faith and vision, for we do not now know what benefits await us.

113
00:14:54,100 --> 00:14:58,740
But if I were to say, my fellow citizens, that we shall send to the moon

114
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:09,539
240,000 miles away from the control station in Houston, a giant rocket more than 300 feet tall,

115
00:15:09,539 --> 00:15:15,620
the length of this football field, made of new metal alloys, some of which have not yet been

116
00:15:15,620 --> 00:15:21,940
invented, capable of standing heat and stresses several times more than, than have ever been

117
00:15:21,940 --> 00:15:28,659
experienced, fitted together with a precision better than the finest watch, carrying all the

118
00:15:28,659 --> 00:15:34,740
equipment needed for propulsion, guidance, control, communication, food and survival,

119
00:15:35,299 --> 00:15:42,019
on an untried mission to an unknown celestial body, and then return it safely to Earth,

120
00:15:42,820 --> 00:15:50,019
re-entering the atmosphere at speeds of over 25,000 miles per hour, causing heat about half that of

121
00:15:50,019 --> 00:15:56,580
the temperature of the sun, almost as hot as it is here today, and do all this, and do all this,

122
00:15:56,580 --> 00:16:02,419
and do it right, and do it first, before this decade is out, then we must be bold.

123
00:16:12,450 --> 00:16:18,769
I'm the one who's doing all the work, so we just want you to stay cool for a minute. However, I think

124
00:16:18,769 --> 00:16:24,129
we're going to do it, and I think that we must pay what needs to be paid. I don't think we ought

125
00:16:24,129 --> 00:16:28,850
to waste any money, but I think we ought to do the job, and this will be done for the decade of the

126
00:16:28,850 --> 00:16:33,730
sixties. It may be done, while some of you are still here at school, at this college and university,

127
00:16:34,289 --> 00:16:38,610
it will be done during the terms of office of some of the people who sit here on this

128
00:16:38,610 --> 00:16:43,250
platform, but it will be done, and it will be done before the end of this decade,

129
00:16:43,250 --> 00:16:48,529
and I am delighted that this university is playing a part in putting a man on the moon

130
00:16:48,529 --> 00:16:52,450
as part of a great national effort of the United States of America.

131
00:16:52,450 --> 00:17:07,869
Many years ago, the great British explorer George Mallory, who was to die on Mount Everest,

132
00:17:07,869 --> 00:17:13,950
was asked why did he want to climb it. He said because it is there, well space is there,

133
00:17:13,950 --> 00:17:17,470
and we're going to climb it, and the moon and the planets are there,

134
00:17:18,029 --> 00:17:22,670
and new hopes for knowledge and peace are there, and therefore, as we set sail,

135
00:17:23,630 --> 00:17:29,549
we ask God's blessing on the most hazardous and dangerous and greatest adventure

136
00:17:29,549 --> 00:17:32,190
on which man is ever involved. Thank you.

